<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Takeda clan</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Takeda were a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> clan descended from the Minamoto that ruled Kai Province from the 12th century. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takeda-shingen">Takeda Shingen</a> in the 16th century they became one of the strongest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sengoku-period">Sengoku</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a>, conquering Shinano and parts of Suruga and Tōtōmi and fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uesugi-kenshin">Uesugi Kenshin</a>. Shingen's son Katsuyori was defeated at Nagashino in 1575, and the clan was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> in 1582.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/takeda-clan.jpg" alt="Takeda clan" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mukai · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Japanese_Crest_Takeda_Hisi.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
